WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.438
OpenAPI documentation can help engineering
teams, client developers,

2
00:00:07.518 --> 00:00:13.774
support teams, and security reviewers
understand a Minimal API.

3
00:00:13.854 --> 00:00:20.110
Accurate documentation shows routes,
methods, request shapes,

4
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:27.925
response shapes, authentication
expectations, authorization expectations,

5
00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:29.938
and error behavior.

6
00:00:30.018 --> 00:00:36.501
That makes review easier when the
documentation reflects reality.

7
00:00:36.581 --> 00:00:44.657
Documentation should match real
authentication and authorization requirements.

8
00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:47.922
If an endpoint requires a policy,

9
00:00:48.002 --> 00:00:55.054
the documentation should make that
expectation visible where possible.

10
00:00:55.134 --> 00:00:58.660
If a schema represents a request DTO,

11
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.427
it should include only fields clients are
supposed to send.

12
00:01:04.507 --> 00:01:08.147
If a response schema contains fields,

13
00:01:08.227 --> 00:01:13.687
those fields should be safe for the
documented audience.

14
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:20.477
Internal endpoints, administrative
endpoints, preview endpoints,

15
00:01:20.557 --> 00:01:25.903
and sensitive metadata should not be
exposed casually.

16
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:34.400
Development documentation settings should
not silently become production exposure.

17
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:41.874
Some applications can safely expose
selected documentation in production;

18
00:01:41.954 --> 00:01:49.006
others should restrict it to internal
users, development environments,

19
00:01:49.086 --> 00:01:51.929
or authenticated audiences.

20
00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:56.331
The decision should be owned and
documented.

21
00:01:56.411 --> 00:02:04.601
Documented schemas should not reveal
sensitive fields that clients should not use or

22
00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:05.136
see.

23
00:02:05.216 --> 00:02:12.723
If an internal model appears in OpenAPI,
it may expose implementation detail,

24
00:02:12.803 --> 00:02:21.106
hidden flags, administrative values,
tenant markers, or server-controlled fields.

25
00:02:21.186 --> 00:02:27.556
DTOs help keep documentation aligned with
the intended contract.

26
00:02:27.636 --> 00:02:32.982
OpenAPI is a security review aid, not an
access control.

27
00:02:33.062 --> 00:02:37.953
Hiding documentation does not secure an
endpoint,

28
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:43.379
and showing documentation does not
authorize a caller.

29
00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:49.828
The API still needs authentication,
authorization, validation,

30
00:02:49.908 --> 00:02:54.572
safe responses, request controls, and
logging.

31
00:02:54.652 --> 00:03:02.727
Production documentation should have
environment rules and review expectations.

32
00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:09.632
Teams should know whether documentation is
enabled, who can access it,

33
00:03:09.712 --> 00:03:14.148
how it is protected, how schemas are
reviewed,

34
00:03:14.228 --> 00:03:19.460
and how preview endpoints are handled
before release.
